SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Hate Crime Statistics","publisher":"U.S. Department of Justice","publication_date":"2023-10","url":"https://www.justice.gov/hatecrimes/2022-hate-crime-statistics","claim":"The FBI UCR program reported 11,634 hate-crime incidents and 13,337 offenses in 2022, with most single-bias incidents motivated by race/ethnicity/ancestry and many incidents occurring near residences or homes.","locator":"lines 128-129, 153-157, 176-177","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official federal crime-statistics page summarizing FBI UCR data; strong for baseline domestic-security context but limited by voluntary reporting.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 conditions, released in the 2022 statistics cycle.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect. This reflects the domestic safety environment during Biden's term, but it does not by itself show Biden caused or prevented the underlying violence.","contrary_evidence":["Law-enforcement participation is voluntary, so underreporting is likely."],"lenses":["3B.7","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Justice Department Launches Nationwide Initiative to Combat Unlawful Acts of Hate","publisher":"U.S. Department of Justice","publication_date":"2022-09-15","url":"https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-launches-nationwide-initiative-combat-unlawful-acts-hate","claim":"DOJ announced that all 94 U.S. Attorneys' Offices would host United Against Hate outreach and community-engagement programs to strengthen reporting and trust around hate crimes.","locator":"lines 139-144","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct DOJ announcement of a concrete nationwide rollout with named offices and implementation timeline.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year action in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"High. This was a Biden-administration DOJ initiative carried out under the Attorney General's authority.","contrary_evidence":["Outreach can improve reporting without proving that hate-crime incidence fell."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.7","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Remarks Honoring the Victims of the Shooting at the Tops Friendly Markets Grocery Store and Condemning White Supremacy in Buffalo, New York","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-05-17","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/remarks-honoring-the-victims-the-shooting-the-tops-friendly-markets-grocery-store-and","claim":"Biden publicly condemned white supremacy, called the Buffalo massacre domestic terrorism, and urged Americans to reject the replacement-theory lie and the ideology behind it.","locator":"lines 95-101, 106-108","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential transcript with precise attribution and contemporaneous timing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022, immediately after the Buffalo attack.","ruler_attribution":"High. These are Biden's own public words in response to a major domestic terror event.","contrary_evidence":["Rhetoric alone does not establish durable safety improvements or enforcement outcomes."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Assistant Attorney General Kristen Clarke Announces Consent Decree with City of Springfield, Massachusetts","publisher":"United States Department of Justice","publication_date":"2022-04-13","url":"https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/speech/assistant-attorney-general-kristen-clarke-announces-consent-decree-city-springfield","claim":"DOJ said its Springfield investigation found systemic problems that led to a pattern or practice of excessive force, with deficiencies in policies, training, and accountability allowing violent force to go unreported and unchecked.","locator":"lines 148-149","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct DOJ civil-rights finding and announcement tied to a court-enforceable reform process.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Medium-high. The federal enforcement action is attributable to Biden's DOJ, but the underlying misconduct was local and predated the decree.","contrary_evidence":["The misconduct was municipal, not national, and the decree responds to rather than proves nationwide control."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.8","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DOJ launches consent decree with Springfield, Mass., police","publisher":"The Washington Post","publication_date":"2022-04-13","url":"https://www.washingtonpost.com/national-security/2022/04/13/springfield-police-consent-doj/","claim":"The Springfield agreement would require officers to report all force, create a force investigation team, and place the department under a federal monitor.","locator":"line 27","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent contemporaneous reporting that adds implementation detail beyond the DOJ announcement.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Medium-high. This corroborates Biden-era federal enforcement, but it remains a local department reform rather than a direct presidential command.","contrary_evidence":["Local reform does not by itself prove national-level control over all police conduct."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Law Enforcement: DHS Should Strengthen Use of Force Data Collection and Analysis","publisher":"U.S. Government Accountability Office","publication_date":"2023-07-24","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-23-105927","claim":"GAO found DHS began collecting use-of-force data in May 2022, but agencies sometimes combined multiple uses of force into one incident, undercounting actual force events, and DHS still lacked a plan for analyzing the data.","locator":"lines 266-269, 282-289","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent legislative-branch oversight with explicit findings and recommendations on 2022 DHS practice.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Assesses FY2021-FY2022 practice and the 2022 policy environment.","ruler_attribution":"Medium. The report reflects Biden-era DHS policy and oversight architecture, but it evaluates component practice rather than a direct presidential order.","contrary_evidence":["A later report cannot by itself prove that 2022 use-of-force incidents were adequately handled in real time."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.5","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Justice Department and Department of Homeland Security Issue Rule to Efficiently and Fairly Process Asylum Claims","publisher":"United States Department of Justice","publication_date":"2022-03-24","url":"https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/justice-department-and-department-homeland-security-issue-rule-efficiently-and-fairly-process","claim":"DOJ and DHS issued a rule to improve and expedite asylum-claim processing for people subject to expedited removal, aiming to grant relief quickly to eligible asylum seekers and promptly remove those who are not eligible.","locator":"lines 138-138","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct rule announcement from the agencies implementing the policy.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"High for Biden-era executive-branch policy; the rule is a direct product of the administration's immigration apparatus.","contrary_evidence":["Broader border-policy critics argued that faster processing did not resolve the underlying limits on asylum access."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.5","3B.7","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Federal Register Notices - 2022","publisher":"Executive Office for Immigration Review, U.S. Department of Justice","publication_date":"2022-10-19","url":"https://www.justice.gov/eoir/federal-register-notices-2022","claim":"The October 19, 2022 DHS notice created a safe and orderly parole pathway for certain Venezuelans with U.S. supporters and public-safety vetting, while stating that unauthorized entrants between ports of entry would be subject to expulsion or removal.","locator":"lines 429-431","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Federal Register summary of the DHS notice with the operative policy language and eligibility conditions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"High. This is a Biden-administration DHS decision and implementation mechanism on border management.","contrary_evidence":["Human-rights groups argued the same policy expanded expulsions and limited asylum access."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.7","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"US/Mexico: Expelling Venezuelans Threatens Rights, Lives","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-10-21","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/10/21/us/mexico-expelling-venezuelans-threatens-rights-lives","claim":"HRW said the Biden and AMLO decision to expand Title 42 expulsions for Venezuelans without allowing asylum access put lives at risk and violated international law.","locator":"line 607","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Credible rights-monitoring report, but it is advocacy-oriented and evaluates policy harm rather than internal executive intent.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Medium-high for policy consequences; low for proving intent beyond the public border decision.","contrary_evidence":["DHS framed the parallel process as a safe, orderly, and vetted legal pathway."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.7","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"U.S. and Mexico reach agreement on plans for Venezuelan migrants","publisher":"The Washington Post","publication_date":"2022-10-12","url":"https://www.washingtonpost.com/national-security/2022/10/12/united-states-mexico-venezuelans/","claim":"The U.S.-Mexico plan would let U.S. authorities send some Venezuelan migrants back across the border while expanding legal entry opportunities for others through an application process abroad.","locator":"lines 24-26","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent contemporaneous reporting that captures the enforcement-and-parole structure of the policy.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"High for the policy choice itself, but the article does not prove downstream safety outcomes.","contrary_evidence":["A legal-entry channel was paired with expulsions, so the policy was mixed rather than purely protective."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.7","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Attorney General Merrick B. Garland Announces Justice Department Strategy to Combat Human Trafficking","publisher":"United States Department of Justice","publication_date":"2022-01-31","url":"https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/attorney-general-merrick-b-garland-announces-justice-department-strategy-combat-human","claim":"DOJ released a 2022 national strategy to combat human trafficking, rooted in President Biden's National Action Plan, with federally funded local task forces, victim screening protocols, and expanded victim-centered assistance.","locator":"lines 141-154","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct DOJ strategy announcement with concrete program elements and implementation direction.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"High. This is a Biden-administration strategy with clear federal ownership and implementation mechanisms.","contrary_evidence":["A strategy document shows intent and architecture more than realized victim outcomes."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.7","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"FBI Announces Results of Nationwide Sex Trafficking Operation","publisher":"United States Department of Justice","publication_date":"2022-08-15","url":"https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/fbi-announces-results-nationwide-sex-trafficking-operation","claim":"The FBI's Operation Cross Country XII identified and located 84 minor victims of child sex trafficking and 37 actively missing children during a two-week nationwide enforcement campaign.","locator":"lines 139-143","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct DOJ/FBI enforcement report with concrete output counts and named operation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"High for federal enforcement performance under the Biden DOJ, though the operation is episodic rather than a full-system outcome measure.","contrary_evidence":["An enforcement campaign is not the same as sustained year-round protection from trafficking."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.7","3B.10"]}

Gap status:
- Direct national-level control over local policing and border enforcement: partly resolved.
- Independent 2022 outcome data on accountability mechanisms and protest treatment: partly resolved.

Search ledger:
- Candidates found: ~39
- Documents opened: 22
- Duplicates removed: 9
- Irrelevant or out-of-period items: 5
- Inaccessible items: 4
- Accepted distinct URLs: 12

Blockers:
- Several DHS archive pages returned `403 Forbidden`, including the 2023 DHS use-of-force policy page.
- PBS pages for Buffalo coverage also returned `403 Forbidden`, so I used the primary presidential transcript instead.
- One Reuters mirror returned an internal fetch error, so I relied on other accessible independent coverage.
- The remaining gap is not full national control over decentralized policing; the evidence now shows federal oversight and remedies, not complete operational command.
